  "metadata": {
    "title": "Shape transitions and rare reversals in the elephant random walk",
    "author": "Arjun Pemmasani",
    "primary_category": "math.PR",
    "cross_list": "cond-mat.stat-mech",
    "optional_cross_list": "math.CO",
    "abstract": "At every superdiffusive memory $a$, the initial biases for which the elephant random walk's limit density is bimodal form an open interval centred at $1/2$, empty exactly when $a\\le a_*$. Outside it, the density is unimodal with a unique local maximum. The proof controls first differences, their boundary histories and the dominant half of each finite count law; every finite law has at most two modal plateaus. For a fair first step, the density has two inflection points when unimodal and four when bimodal. A certified computation proves a unique modal transition at $a_*\\approx0.6688128600146$, enclosed in an interval shorter than $3.3\\cdot10^{-15}$, with the critical density unimodal. Bimodality for $a\\ge4/5$ also has a proof without computation. As $a\\to1$, a two-component Landau approximation holds uniformly over the initial bias, in total variation and scaled uniform density distance. It determines the locations and heights of the fair-start peaks. For rare reversals, an explicit total variation approximation by a truncated Luria--Delbr\\\"uck law gives a Landau local limit theorem with a rate. When $\\log N\\le x=N\\varepsilon\\le2\\log N$, every mode lies at distance $x\\log x+\\lambda_{\\mathrm L}x+O(\\sqrt{x\\log x})$ from an end. For even $N$, the origin and each end are equally likely at a unique reversal probability, with $N\\varepsilon=2\\log N-\\log\\log N-\\log16+o(1)$. At this crossing, every even $N\\ge8$ has two symmetric modal intervals and a unique interior minimum, by finite certified computations and analytic bounds.",
